The C++ layer driving brotli, lz4, lz5, lizard and fast-lzma2 - 22 encoder,
decoder and handler files under CPP/7zip/Compress/ and CPP/7zip/Archive/ - was
ported from mcmilk/7-Zip-zstd, Copyright (c) Tino Reichardt. That project is
itself a fork of 7-Zip and states its license as "the same as the Mainline
7-Zip, which means most of the code is GNU LGPL v2.1-or-later", which is what
permits redistribution here under LGPL v3. Each ported file says so in its own
header, so this lists exactly those 22 and nothing else:

    git grep -l "Ported from mcmilk/7-Zip-zstd"

Searching for the project's name instead is misleading: seven further files
under CPP/ mention 7-Zip-zstd without being ports of it - the five
*Register.cpp files, MethodProps.cpp and 7zip.mak - and they say why. They
match mcmilk's coder ids and property names so that archives interoperate.
Those are this project's own code, or upstream's, not Tino Reichardt's.

The zstd C++ files are not among them. ZstdDecoder.cpp and ZstdHandler.cpp are
upstream 7-Zip's own (upstream ships zstd decoding); ZstdEncoder.cpp,
ZstdEncoderProps.* and ZstdRegister.cpp were written for this project against
the encoder API upstream had already stubbed out. They deliberately match
7-Zip-zstd's `.7z` coder id (0x4F71101) and its five-byte coder-properties
layout, so archives interoperate - that is format compatibility, not shared
code.

